{"author":"rebar-bridge[bot]","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"compacted_at":1793168319969639166,"compiled_state":{"alias":"affordable-omphacite-sunfish","assignee":null,"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","authorship":{"signed":2,"unsigned":0},"authorship_ledger":[{"content_hash":"5de29c9f37091c1bd73bb3c508b4b9583ba789d637734aa44795aca7d22687b8","event_uuid":"5b89559e-41e1-4fe5-8626-7fdf39d97fe3","position":{"commit_sha":"68b4f97477b8d9f57845d4457e869def25487eaa","position":"1793152504818246261-5b89559e-41e1-4fe5-8626-7fdf39d97fe3"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null},{"content_hash":"6a89b3725aa146dfdd480918d27e98bc1afcd039edca95fb0fccb4dc381af146","event_uuid":"2437c61b-cbb2-4e18-9f0a-37eea85dbb72","position":{"commit_sha":"26e842ba65bd424a9ca0d59a1b7af774ccb95c62","position":"1793162180624783043-2437c61b-cbb2-4e18-9f0a-37eea85dbb72"},"signature":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":null}],"bridge_alerts":[],"bridge_project":null,"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"## Root cause identified — hypothesis 1 confirmed, with the exact writer named\n\nThe `.git/config` rewrite at 17:36 was performed by a **concurrent Claude Code session (PID 12747, started 17:01:41 PDT)**, via a shell it spawned at **17:36:09 PDT** — matching the recorded `.git/config` mtime to the minute.\n\n### Evidence\n\n`ps -Ao pid,ppid,lstart,command` run 2026-10-27 21:34 PDT shows PID 30648 (parent 12747), started `Tue Oct 27 17:36:09 2026`, running:\n\n```sh\nsc=$(ls -d $PWD/.rebar/scratch/df72-*) && \\\nfor r in $(git -C $sc/tracker-copy remote); do git -C $sc/tracker-copy remote remove $r; done\n```\n\n`$PWD` was the worktree `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.rebar/worktrees/fix-df72-stale-snapshot-close`, so `$sc` = `.../.rebar/scratch/df72-23558`.\n\nThe intent was clearly to strip remotes from a *throwaway copy* of the tracker. It did not do that. `tracker-copy` was produced by a **file copy of the real tracker worktree**, and that copy carried the real worktree's `.git` **file** verbatim:\n\n```\n$ cat .../df72-23558/tracker-copy/.git\ngitdir: /Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker\n\n$ git -C .../df72-23558/tracker-copy rev-parse --git-dir --git-common-dir\n/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/worktrees/-tickets-tracker\n/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git\n```\n\nThat path is **absolute**. A copied worktree is not a copy of a repository — it is a second pointer at the original. So `git -C tracker-copy remote` listed the SHARED repository's remotes (`origin`, `gerrit`), and `git remote remove` wrote to the SHARED `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar/.git/config`. That is exactly the observed damage: both `[remote \"...\"]` sections gone, `[core]`, `[gc]`, `[maintenance]`, `[merge \"ours\"]`, `[user]`, `[credential]` all intact, because `git remote remove` edits only the sections it owns.\n\n### Status of the three recorded hypotheses\n\n1. **Concurrent session / operator action** — CONFIRMED, with the process, its parent, its start time, and its command line.\n2. **A tool that rewrites `.git/config` wholesale** — REFUTED. Nothing rewrote the file wholesale; `git remote remove` surgically deleted two sections, which is why everything else survived. The surgical-survival pattern that looked like \"a tool that did not model those sections\" is just git's own section editing.\n3. **Related to the 18:40 `git timed out after 30s` lock fault** — correlation only, and now explainable: the same runaway process (see below) holds a rebar write lock and runs git operations against the shared object store, so git contention on this host at that time is expected rather than coincidental.\n\n### This is not isolated — the same process also caused bug `painted-epidermal-shelduck` (`f1a2-0755-693d-4924`)\n\nThe same shell then ran `converge_snapshot_close_metadata_unit(tracker-copy)` under `REBAR_SYNC_PUSH=off REBAR_SYNC_PULL=off`. Because `tracker-copy` resolves to the real tracker's git-dir with `HEAD -> refs/heads/tickets`, its REBUILD SNAPSHOT commits landed on the **real local `tickets` branch** and were never pushed. The `tickets` reflog shows exactly 4 such commits (18:56:20, 19:43:57, 20:03:05, 20:53:16) with no intervening `reset: moving to origin/tickets` — which is the 4-ahead/32-behind divergence that ticket reports.\n\n**The process is still running as of 21:35 PDT (elapsed 3h58m)**, with child PID 30670 still executing `run_unit.py`. It belongs to another session; this session has not signalled it. It may make further commits to the shared `tickets` branch.\n\n### What this means for the fix\n\nThe AC \"identify the writer\" is met. The remaining ACs are prevention, and the generalisable defect is now sharper than the original framing: **copying a linked git worktree directory produces a second live handle on the original repository, not an isolated copy.** Any \"work on a throwaway copy of the tracker\" recipe built with `cp -r` is unsafe by construction — it can rewrite the shared config, move shared refs, and take shared locks. A `make worktree` preflight that names a missing `origin` is still worth having, but it treats the symptom; the durable guard is making the shared checkout's remote configuration recoverable and warning about copied-worktree handles.\n\nNext: proposing the concrete remediation on this ticket before claiming.","timestamp":1793162180624783043}],"created_at":1793152504818246261,"creation_channel":"mcp","deps":[],"description":"## Observation\nAt 2026-10-27 ~18:52 PDT, `git fetch origin` in the shared checkout `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar` failed:\n\n```\nfatal: 'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\n```\n\n`git remote -v` returned **empty** — from the main checkout and from every linked worktree, since they share one config. Inspection:\n\n- `.git/config` was 403 bytes with **no `[remote \"origin\"]` and no `[remote \"gerrit\"]` section**.\n- `.git/refs/remotes/` did not exist.\n- `.git/logs/refs/remotes/` existed but was **empty** — the remote-tracking reflogs were gone too.\n- `grep -c refs/remotes .git/packed-refs` returned `0`.\n- `.git/config` mtime was **Oct 27 17:36**, inside this session's window.\n\nWhat SURVIVED in `.git/config`: `[core]`, `[gc] autoDetach = false`, `[maintenance] autoDetach = false`, `[merge \"ours\"] driver = true`, `[user]` (the bot identity), and a `[credential \"https://github.com\"]` helper. So the file was not truncated or emptied — it was rewritten minus the two remote sections while rebar's own ADR-0051 gc/maintenance settings and the identity survived.\n\n## Impact\nThis blocks ALL code work in the repository, and does so at the two points an agent reaches first:\n\n- `make worktree name=<x>` and any `git worktree add … origin/main` fail — no `origin` to branch from. This is the documented entry point for every change in `AGENTS.md`.\n- `git push gerrit HEAD:refs/for/main` cannot run — no `gerrit`. Nothing can be sent for review.\n\nIt is also silent until used: nothing warns that the remotes went away, and the failure message (\"'origin' does not appear to be a git repository\") reads like a network or permissions problem rather than a missing config section, which sends an investigator down the wrong path.\n\n## Not established — the cause\nI did not modify `.git/config`, and nothing in this session's command history writes to it. Earlier in the same session, pushes to `gerrit` succeeded repeatedly (Gerrit changes 3119, 3120, 3121, 3122, 3123, the last merging at ~14:50 PDT), so both remotes existed and worked for hours before 17:36.\n\nCandidate causes, none confirmed:\n1. A concurrent agent session or operator action on this shared host rewrote the config. The repo is explicitly shared — `AGENTS.md` warns the main checkout holds operator WIP and that linked worktrees share one repo-global config.\n2. A tool that rewrites `.git/config` wholesale (some credential-helper or identity-management steps do) dropped sections it did not model.\n3. Related to a separate fault seen minutes earlier at ~18:40: a local `rebar edit` failed with `git commit failed while holding lock: git timed out after 30s`, suggesting git operations on this host were already degraded. That write did NOT land (the ticket was unchanged), and the same edit succeeded through the MCP server.\n\nHypotheses 1 and 2 are untested; 3 is correlation in time only.\n\n## Recovery applied (verified, not guessed)\n```sh\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/navapbc/rebar.git\ngit remote add gerrit https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/a/rebar\n```\n\nThe URLs were NOT recalled blind. `gerrit` is the host printed verbatim by this session's own push output (`To https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/a/rebar`). `origin` was then proven by fetching and checking that a commit this session pushed and merged is present and is the branch tip:\n\n```\ngit fetch origin && git log --oneline -1 origin/main\nee5253abfd4 Document the eval harnesses for client projects\n```\n\nA wrong URL would have failed the fetch or lacked that object, so this is empirical confirmation rather than assumption.\n\n## Proposed work\n1. Determine the writer. Check shell history, any config-rewriting tooling, and whether a sibling agent session was active at 17:36. Until that is known, the recovery above is a repair, not a fix — it can recur at any moment, including mid-push.\n2. Decide whether the remotes should be recorded somewhere recoverable. Today the only record was the config that was lost; a `git remote -v` snapshot in the repo (or a documented restore step in `docs/local-dev-env.md`) turns a blocking mystery into a one-line fix.\n3. Consider a preflight in `make worktree`: when `origin` is absent, fail with \"the repository has no `origin` remote — see <runbook>\" rather than letting raw git emit a message that implies a network fault.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The writer of the 17:36 `.git/config` rewrite is identified, or the investigation records exactly which sources were checked and found silent.\n- [ ] The canonical `origin` and `gerrit` URLs are recorded in a tracked file or runbook, so recovery does not depend on scrollback.\n- [ ] `make worktree` fails with a message naming the missing remote and pointing at the restore step, instead of surfacing raw git's \"does not appear to be a git repository\".\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0.","env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","file_impact":[],"file_impact_scope":"undeclared","keyring":[],"managed_refs":[],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":null,"parent_status_uuid":"","plan_review_phase":"planning","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":2,"repos":[],"reverts":[],"status":"open","tags":[],"ticket_id":"1712-f158-9314-420a","ticket_type":"bug","title":"Both git remotes (origin and gerrit) vanished from the shared checkout's .git/config mid-session, blocking all worktree creation and every Gerrit push","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["5b89559e-41e1-4fe5-8626-7fdf39d97fe3","2437c61b-cbb2-4e18-9f0a-37eea85dbb72"]},"env_id":"","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1793168319969639166,"uuid":"c8a91bcf-6b82-4bc5-bd96-99a9b1f56025"}